<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mongol invasions of the Levant</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1240">1240</a>–<a href="/years/1312">1312</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mongol invasions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> were the attacks on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palestine">Palestine</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> and its successor, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a>, from the 1240s to the early 14th century. Hülegü's army sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1260, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Ain Jalut</a> later that year. Further Ilkhanid invasions were turned back, notably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> in 1281 and Marj al-Saffar in 1303, and the Mamluks kept Syria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongol-invasions-of-the-levant.jpg" alt="Mongol invasions of the Levant" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Map Master · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mongol_raids_in_Syria_and_Palestine_1260.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Mongol expansion rested on the idea that the eternal sky had destined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> to rule the world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a>, a son of Tolui and loyal to his brother Kublai, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berke">Berke</a> disputed his conquests south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. In 1261 Berke allied with the Mamluk Sultan Baibars against the Ilkhanate, and Golden Horde attacks in the north repeatedly forced the Ilkhanate to recall troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> within months. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> of the Ilkhanate sought an alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> without success.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>A first raid under Yisaur in 1244 reached Hailan near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, whose ruler agreed to pay tribute, and in 1251 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nasir-yusuf">An-Nasir Yusuf</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>. After sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1258, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a>'s army, joined by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgians">Georgians</a>, the army of Cilician Armenia under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hethum-i">Hethum I</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemond-vi-of-antioch">Bohemond VI of Antioch</a>, took Aleppo in January 1260, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kitbuqa">Kitbuqa</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> on 1 March. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a>'s death called Hulagu away, about 10,000 horsemen remained; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> defeated them at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ain-jalut">Ain Jalut</a> in September 1260 and executed Kitbuqa.</p><p>Samagar took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in October 1271 but retreated before Baibars, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abaqa-khan">Abaqa Khan</a>'s army of 40,000 was beaten at the Second Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> in October 1281. In December 1299 Ghazan routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> at the Battle of Wadi al-Khazandar and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, though not its citadel, but withdrew in February 1300, perhaps because the Chagatai Mongols threatened his eastern border or for want of grazing, which the Mamluks denied him by burning pastures. His next invasion ended in defeat at Marj al-Saffar in April 1303, and Öljaitü's siege of al-Rahba in 1312 failed after about a month.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>As the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, peace became possible, and under Abu Sa'id, advised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chupan">Chupan</a>, a treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> was ratified in 1322/1323. The invasions brought upheaval and migration. Bar Hebraeus wrote that the entire population of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 1259, and the pro-Mongol historian Rashīd al-Dīn that whole provinces were killed or fled. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harran">Harran</a>, al-Ruha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saruj">Saruj</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raqqa">Raqqa</a> were left abandoned, and many towns stayed empty for centuries. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and Syria lost, the centre of Islamic power passed to the Mamluks in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mongol invasions of Syria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
